    {
      "slug": "reddit-heat-wave-dress-code",
      "script": "Wool at ninety-one degrees? My manager wrote me up for wearing a short-sleeve polo after our store's air conditioner broke during a brutal heat wave. He said if I couldn't dress professionally, I shouldn't clock in. So I followed the employee handbook exactly. Next day: pressed slacks, a collared dress shirt, a tie, plus the wool blazer nobody had worn since the store opened. Page forty had spoken. Customers offered water while sweat soaked through the blazer, and I loudly explained that corporate required every stitch. During my third sweltering hour, our regional manager walked in, saw my soaked collar, and asked why the air conditioner had been broken for eleven days. A technician arrived within two hours. The handbook gained an official heat-wave exception—with my name on the memo. Was page forty worth eleven days?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a four-word question hook, tightened the handbook-versus-heat escalation, added one dry page-forty aside, and landed on eleven days while preserving every fact and shot phrase."
    },
    {
      "slug": "reddit-lunch-thief-ghost-pepper",
      "script": "Who stole three sandwiches? Someone kept stealing my lunch from the shared fridge. After three sandwiches vanished in two weeks, I set a trap. I bought the hottest ghost pepper sauce, layered it inside a turkey sandwich that looked like mine, wrote my name on the bag, and left it on the middle shelf. By eleven that morning: coughing violently in the break room, chugging every cup of water, mouth on fire. My actual lunch was still sealed in my desk drawer. The thief was our composed office manager—red-faced, eyes streaming, unable to speak. She never confessed, but started bringing her own lunch every single day. Our supervisor posted a handwritten sign above the fridge warning that stolen food would not be replaced. Nobody has touched my sandwich since. Was one stolen turkey sandwich worth it?",
      "editor_note": "Punched up to a four-word question hook, compressed the sandwich-trap escalation, kept the injury-related sequence straight, and landed on the stolen turkey sandwich while preserving every fact and shot phrase."
    }
